  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nA strong cold front associated with a low pressure system in Canada \nis pushing through eastern and southeastern IL currently, bringing \nmuch cooler temperatures and rain with it. Thunderstorms are \ncontinued to not be expected. Rainfall amounts won't be drought \nbusting. We have seen radar estimates so far of stripes of 0.25-0.5 \ninches with other areas seeing less than 0.25 inch. The rain, along \nwith the front, should exit the southeast by 00z this evening. \nLocations along and west of I-55 are in the clear of the rain. \n\nBehind the cold front, winds have shifted out of the northwest, \nushering in much cooler temperatures that will continue for the next \nweek. Highs in the low to mid 40s for the first half of the week, \nslightly warming up to mid 40s to low 50s for the end of the week. \nLows will be in the 20s each night this week. Take care to have \nplenty of warm bedding for outdoor animals/livestock and bring in \nany vulnerable plants if they hadn't already been impacted by the \nlast round of freezing temperatures. \n\nDry weather will be in place for the first few days of the new week, \nbut a complex system looks to be brewing for Wednesday into \nThursday, potentially impacting holiday travel. There is still high \nuncertainty with the mid week system. The synoptic models are \nstarting to show a similar story with a more western track of the \nprecipitation. We shall see if that trend continues with each \nadditional run. Moisture will be drawn north by a low pressure \ncenter in the southern US. Lift will be provided by the upper \nshortwave passing over, along with a band of frontogenesis set up \nover IL. \n\nCurrently, it looks like rain and snow will accompany this system. \nDaytime temperatures are in the low 40s keeping p-type as rain, but \nthe nighttime temperatures are in the 20s, allowing a snow switch \nover. One concern would be the possibility that any liquid/melted \nsnow on the roads could freeze on the roadways. The 13z NBM is \nshowing ~20-40% chance of 1 inch of snow for areas along and north \nof I-70 from 12z Thursday to 12z Friday. Timing of the system is \nstill uncertain but the models are generally showing the precip \nmoving in from the south Wednesday evening. The greater timing \nuncertainty comes from the end time of the event. The GFS has it \nmoving off to the east by Friday morning, whereas, the Euro has it \nlingering into Friday evening. \n\nWe will have to continue to monitor the trends in amounts, timing, \nand locations as we move closer to the holiday. Stay tuned for the \nnewest information with each forecast run, as this is the busiest \ntravel week of the year.",
